The '''2026 Tour de France''' was the 113th edition of the [[Tour de France]]. The race began with a stage in [[Barcelona]], Spain, on 4 July, and finished with the final stage at the [[Champs-Élysées]], [[Paris]], on 26 July.

[[Tadej Pogačar]] of {{UCI team code|UAD men|2026}} won the [[General classification in the Tour de France|general classification]] for the fifth time, and the third year running. His attack on stage 6, at the [[Col du Tourmalet]] in the [[Pyrenees]], established a lead over his main rival [[Jonas Vingegaard]] of {{UCI team code|TVL men|2026}}. Pogačar extended his lead with victories on three mountain stages: stage 10 in the [[Massif Central]], stage 14 in [[Vosges]], and stage 19 to [[Alpe d'Huez]] in the [[French Alps]]. Pogačar's final margin of victory was almost six and a half minutes. It was the fastest race on record, with Pogačar having an average speed of {{Convert|43.227|km/h|mph}} over the three weeks of racing.

Second place overall went to [[Remco Evenepoel]] of {{UCI team code|RBH|2026}}, after Vingegaard withdrew from the race following a crash on stage 15. Evenepoel took his first mountain stage victory at [[Plateau de Solaison]] on stage 15 and then won the [[individual time trial]] to [[Thonon-les-Bains]] in stage 16. Third place went to [[Isaac del Toro]], a Tour debutant. On stage 20, Pogačar assisted his teammate Del Toro, to ensure that Del Toro held off fellow Tour debutant [[Paul Seixas]] of {{UCI team code|DCT|2026}} for third place overall and the [[Young rider classification in the Tour de France|young rider classification]] as the best rider under the age of 26.

The race was affected by a [[2026 European heatwaves|heatwave]], with two stages shortened and rules relaxed to allow for riders to stay hydrated.<ref name=":18">{{Cite web |last=Root |first=Tik |date=27 July 2026 |title=The year climate change came for the Tour de France |url=https://www.motherjones.com/environment/2026/07/the-year-climate-change-came-for-the-tour-de-france/ |access-date=27 July 2026 |website=Mother Jones |language=en-US}}</ref> Prior to stage 15, both Pogačar and Vingegaard were woken in the middle of the night to take [[Doping in cycling|doping tests]], which generated substantial debate.<ref name=":19">{{Cite web |date=19 July 2026 |title=Tour de France 2026: Stage 15 winner Remco Evenepoel criticises "inhuman" middle-of-the night doping tests |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/cycling/articles/c0jl84wp50wo |access-date=26 July 2026 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref>

The [[Points classification in the Tour de France|points classification]] was won by [[Mads Pedersen]] of {{UCI team code|LTK men|2026}}. Pedersen won only one stage ([[2026 Tour de France, Stage 1 to Stage 11#Stage 4|stage 4]]), but consistently placed highly in the intermediate sprints and secured points in other sprint finishes. The [[Mountains classification in the Tour de France|mountains classification]] went to [[Richard Carapaz]] of {{UCI team code|EFE|2025}}, who won two mountain stages in the French Alps in the last week. Carapaz also won the award for the race's most combative rider. The [[Team classification in the Tour de France|teams classification]] was won by {{UCI team code|LTK men|2026|nolink=y}}.

== Teams ==
{{Main|List of teams and cyclists in the 2026 Tour de France}}
Twenty-three teams took part in the race. All 18 [[UCI WorldTeam]]s were automatically invited. They were joined by five [[UCI ProSeries|UCI ProTeams]]: the three highest ranked UCI ProTeams in 2025 ({{UCI team code|TUD|2026}}, {{UCI team code|Q36|2026}} and {{UCI team code|COF men|2025}}), along with two teams ({{UCI team code|TEN|2026}} and {{UCI team code|CJR|2026}}) selected by [[Amaury Sport Organisation]] (ASO), the organisers of the Tour.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web|title=Tour de France 2026 teams and wildcards unveiled, with Spanish team picked for debut over popular Unibet Rose Rockets|url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/teams-riders/tour-de-france-2026-teams-and-wildcards-unveiled-with-spanish-team-picked-for-debut-over-popular-unibet-rose-rockets/|website=Cyclingnews|date=30 January 2026|access-date=30 January 2026|language=en|first=James|last=Moultrie}}</ref> The teams were announced on 30 January 2026.<ref name=":4" /> {{UCI team code|URR|2026}} had expected to be invited, however Caja Rural-Seguros RGA was chosen instead – which Tour de France director [[Christian Prudhomme]] explained was due to Caja Rural-Seguros RGA's performances in 2025, which included finishing fourth in the team classification at the [[2025 Vuelta a España]].<ref name=":4" /><ref>{{Cite web|title="They don't claim to be French at all" – Tour de France director Prudhomme explains Unibet Rose Rockets snub and Caja Rural selection|url=https://cyclinguptodate.com/cycling/they-dont-claim-to-be-french-at-all-tour-de-france-director-prudhomme-explains-unibet-rose-rockets-snub-and-caja-rural-selection|website=CyclingUpToDate.com|date=30 January 2026|access-date=30 January 2026|language=en|last=Silva|first=Rúben}}</ref>

