A total of 184 riders from 27 nationalities participated in the race, with Belgium having the largest contingent (31 riders).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Number of competitors per country competing in Tour de France 2026. |url=https://www.procyclingstats.com/race/tour-de-france/2026/startlist/competitors-per-country |access-date=3 July 2026 |website=Procyclingstats}}</ref>

== Route and stages==
[[File:Alpe_d'Huez,_France_(7957045090).jpg|thumb|[[2026 Tour de France, Stage 12 to Stage 21|Stage 19]] finished at the top of [[Alpe d'Huez]]; the climb is {{convert|13.8|km|1|abbr=on}} long,  an average gradient of 7.9% and features 21 [[hairpin turns]]. ]]
In February 2025, race organisers ASO announced that [[Barcelona]] in [[Spain]] would host the ''[[List of Tour de France Grands Départs|Grand Départ]]'' (first stage).<ref name=":1">{{cite news|url=https://www.lequipe.fr/Cyclisme-sur-route/Actualites/Un-contre-la-montre-par-equipes-et-une-etape-vallonnee-a-barcelone-pour-lancer-le-tour-de-france-2026/1542674|title=Un contre-la-montre par équipes et une étape vallonnée à Barcelone pour lancer le Tour de France 2026|trans-title=Team time trial and hilly stage in Barcelona to kick off 2026 Tour de France|language=fr|publisher=L'Équipe|date=25 February 2025|access-date=26 February 2025}}</ref> This was the third time the ''Grand Départ'' was held in [[Spain]], after [[San Sebastián]] in 1992 and [[Bilbao]] in 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Grand Départ Barcelona 2026 – Tour de France – Tourism of Barcelona |url=https://www.barcelonaturisme.com/wv3/en/agenda/20589/grand-depart-barcelona-2026-tour-de-france.html |access-date=10 April 2026 |website=www.barcelonaturisme.com |language=en}}</ref> The first two stages finished on the [[Montjuïc]] hill above Barcelona, next to the [[Estadi Olímpic Lluís Companys]] used for the [[1992 Summer Olympics]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Weislo |first=Laura |date=25 February 2025 |title=2026 Tour de France to start with team time trial in Barcelona |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/news/2026-tour-de-france-to-start-with-team-time-trial-in-barcelona/ |access-date=27 February 2025 |website=cyclingnews.com |language=en}}</ref> Following protests against the [[Gaza war]] that disrupted the [[2025 Vuelta a España]], the [[City Council of Barcelona]] expressed a desire to host the ''Grand Départ'' without the [[Israel–Premier Tech]] team, with protestors threatening to disrupt the Tour if they took part.<ref>{{Cite web |last=González |first=Marco |date=16 September 2025 |title=Barcelona no quiere al Israel en la salida del Tour de Francia 2026 |trans-title=Barcelona doesn't want Israel at the start of the 2026 Tour de France |url=https://as.com/ciclismo/tour_francia/barcelona-veta-al-israel-para-la-salida-del-tour-2026-n/ |access-date=18 September 2025 |website=[[Diario AS]] |language=es}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=12 September 2025 |title=Vuelta protesters threaten to disrupt start of next year’s Tour de France |url=https://observer.co.uk/news/sport/article/vuelta-protesters-threaten-to-disrupt-start-of-next-years-tour-de-france |access-date=18 September 2025 |newspaper=[[The Observer]]|language=en}}</ref> The Israel–Premier Tech team subsequently rebranded as NSN Cycling Team for the [[2026 in men's road cycling|2026 season]], and rode under a Swiss license.<ref>{{cite web |last=Moultrie |first=James |date=20 November 2025 |title=Israel-Premier Tech rebrand as NSN Cycling Team for 2026 season, will race under Swiss licence |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/teams-riders/israel-premier-tech-rebrand-as-nsn-cycling-team-for-2026-season-will-race-under-swiss-licence/ |url-status= |archive-url= |archive-date= |access-date=20 November 2025 |website=[[Cyclingnews.com]]}}</ref>

The full route was announced on 23 October 2025 by [[Christian Prudhomme]], who described it as having a [[crescendo]] towards the finish.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Whittle |first=Jeremy |date=23 October 2025 |title=Tour de France unveils 2026 route with double Alpe d’Huez for men and Ventoux debut for women |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2025/oct/23/tour-de-france-femme-2026-route-cycling |access-date=23 October 2025 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Farrand |first=Stephen |date=23 October 2025 |title='It's a Tour in crescendo' – 2026 Tour de France designed to limit early Tadej Pogačar dominance |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/news/its-a-tour-in-crescendo-2026-tour-de-france-designed-to-limit-early-tadej-pogacar-dominance/ |access-date=24 October 2025 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref> There were five summit finishes, including [[Plateau de Solaison]] for the first time and two finishes at Alpe d'Huez.<ref name="route2" /> The race featured only {{convert|26|km|abbr=on}} of [[individual time trial]], with a team time trial starting the Tour for the first time since [[1971 Tour de France|1971]].<ref name="route2" /> The [[team time trial]] returned to the Tour for the first time since [[2019 Tour de France|2019]], and used new rules previously introduced at the [[2023 Paris–Nice]]. In this format, each rider is given an individual finish time, rather than all riders who finish together getting the same time. This format encourages tactical choices by general classification contenders – whether to ride with the rest of their team to save energy, or ride solo to achieve a better time.<ref name=":0" />

[[Cyclist (magazine)|''Cyclist'']] described the route as "ripe for breakaways"<ref>{{Cite web |last=Davidson |first=Robyn |date=24 October 2025 |title=Tour de France 2026 route analysis: Is it anti-dominance or delaying the inevitable? |url=https://www.cyclist.co.uk/in-depth/tour-de-france-2026-route-analysis |access-date=17 December 2025 |website=Cyclist |language=en}}</ref> and [[Rouleur (magazine)|''Rouleur'']] considered that it would suit Pogačar, because "the Slovenian superstar can do everything".<ref>{{Cite web |title=2026 Tour de France promises suspense until the end – but there are few obstacles for Tadej Pogačar |url=https://www.rouleur.cc/blogs/the-rouleur-journal/2026-tour-de-france-promises-suspense-until-the-end-but-there-are-few-obstacles-for-tadej-pogacar |access-date=17 December 2025 |website=Rouleur |language=en}}</ref> [[Ned Boulting]] criticised the route, writing in ''[[Cycling Weekly]]'' that "there's no cobblestones, no obvious crosswind stages, and no gravel. [...] They have denuded this year [which] feels a little bit disappointing".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Shrubsall |first=James |date=23 October 2025 |title='Remco Evenepoel will be rightfully disappointed': Ned Boulting's take on the route of the Tour de France 2026 |url=https://www.cyclingweekly.com/racing/remco-evenepoel-will-be-rightfully-disappointed-ned-boultings-take-on-the-route-of-the-tour-de-france-2026 |access-date=17 December 2025 |website=Cycling Weekly |language=en}}</ref> ''[[Cyclingnews.com|Cycling News]]'' predicted that the pivotal stages would be stage 6 to [[Gavarnie-Gèdre]] (the first high mountain stage of the race in the [[Pyrenees]]), stage 14 to [[Le Markstein]] in the [[Vosges]], the stage 16 individual time trial and the mountainous stages 19 & 20 to Alpe d'Huez in the [[French Alps]]. They described stage 20 as the [[queen stage]], owing to its three [[Hors catégorie|''hors catégorie'']] climbs.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 October 2025 |title=The key stages of the 2026 Tour de France |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/features/tour-de-france-2026-route-analysis-stages-where-the-race-can-be-won-or-lost/ |access-date=10 July 2026 |website=Cyclingnews |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Lamoureux|first=Lyne|last2=|first2=|last3=|date=8 June 2026|title=Tour de France 2026 stage 20 preview|url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/pro-cycling/racing/tour-de-france-2026-stage-20-preview/|access-date=5 July 2026|website=Cyclingnews|language=en|quote=Stage 20 may be the ultimate queen stage in Tour history}}</ref>
